(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-1-nervous-tissue-cbf-quiz"]={"title":"Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow — Anatomy Exam 1","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"What are the two structural divisions of the nervous system, and what does the PNS include?","choices":["CNS (brain and spinal cord) and PNS (everything outside the CNS, including nerves and ganglia)","CNS made up of the brain only; PNS made up of the spinal cord only, entirely separately","Somatic and autonomic divisions only, described structurally rather than functionally","Sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions only, structurally"],"answer":0,"exp":"The two structural divisions are the CNS (brain and spinal cord) and the PNS (everything outside the CNS), with the PNS including nerves (spinal/peripheral and cranial nerves) and ganglia (neuronal cell bodies). — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What are the two cell types that make up nervous tissue?","choices":["Neurons and osteocytes","Only neurons; there is no supporting cell type","Neuroglia and chondrocytes","Neurons and neuroglia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Nervous tissue consists of two cell types: neurons (responsible for transfer and processing of information) and neuroglia (supporting cells that protect the neuron). — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the function of dendrites, per the material?","choices":["Affect another neuron or an effector organ directly","Stimulated by environmental changes or the activities of other cells/neurons","Conduct the nerve impulse toward the axon terminals","Contain the nucleus and mitochondria of the cell body"],"answer":1,"exp":"Dendrites are stimulated by environmental changes or the activities of other cells/neurons. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What does the cell body of a neuron contain?","choices":["Only the axon hillock region of the neuron, a narrow transitional zone near the cell body","The nucleus, mitochondria, ribosomes, and other organelles and inclusions","Only the axon terminals of the neuron, located far from the cell body","Only the myelin sheath covering the neuron's axon, formed by neighboring glial cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cell body contains the nucleus, mitochondria, ribosomes, and other organelles and inclusions. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What does the axon do, and what do axon terminals do?","choices":["The axon is stimulated by environmental changes; axon terminals contain the neuron's nucleus","The axon terminals conduct the impulse; the axon affects the target organ instead","The axon conducts the nerve impulse toward axon terminals; axon terminals affect another neuron or effector organ (muscle or gland)","The axon and the axon terminals are said to perform an identical, fully interchangeable function"],"answer":2,"exp":"The axon conducts the nerve impulse (action potential) toward the axon terminals, which affect another neuron or an effector organ (muscle or gland). — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What are multipolar neurons, and where are they found?","choices":["Have multiple (2+) cellular processes joining the cell body; abundant in both the CNS and PNS","Possess two cellular processes each; account for a few of the special sensory pathways","Possess a single process that later bifurcates; commonly found in the peripheral nervous system","Have no distinguishable axon or dendrites present anywhere on the cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Multipolar neurons have multiple (2+) cellular processes joining the cell body and are abundant in both the central and peripheral parts of the nervous system. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What are pseudounipolar neurons, and where are they common?","choices":["Possess two processes with the cell body positioned between them; found within the special senses","Common in the CNS; have multiple processes joining the cell body directly","Common in the PNS; have one cellular process connected to the cell body that subsequently bifurcates into two processes","Have no distinguishable axon at all; found only within the retina of the eye"],"answer":2,"exp":"Pseudounipolar neurons are common in the PNS and have one cellular process connected to the cell body that subsequently bifurcates into two processes. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What are bipolar neurons, and where are they found?","choices":["Have processes where axons cannot be distinguished from dendrites","Have multiple (2+) processes; abundant in the CNS and PNS generally","Have two cellular processes; make up a few of the special senses","Have one process that bifurcates; common in the PNS"],"answer":2,"exp":"Bipolar neurons have two cellular processes and make up a few of the special senses. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What distinguishes afferent neurons from efferent neurons?","choices":["Afferent and efferent describe neuron shape, not impulse direction","Afferent neurons conduct impulses away from the CNS; efferent neurons conduct impulses toward the CNS","Afferent neurons conduct impulses toward the CNS; efferent neurons conduct impulses away from the CNS","Both conduct impulses in the same direction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Afferent neurons conduct impulses toward the CNS, while efferent neurons conduct impulses away from the CNS. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the difference between somatic and visceral neurons?","choices":["Somatic neurons convey information to and from somatic structures; visceral neurons innervate visceral structures","Somatic and visceral describe impulse direction, not target structure type","Both terms describe the exact same category of neuron entirely, with no functional distinction","Somatic neurons exclusively innervate the heart, while visceral neurons exclusively innervate skeletal muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Somatic neurons convey information to and from somatic structures, while visceral neurons innervate visceral structures. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the axon hillock?","choices":["Bundles of neurofilaments within the axoplasm","Clusters of free ribosomes within the cell body","The cytoplasm contained within the axon","The area where the initial segment connects to the cell body"],"answer":3,"exp":"The axon hillock is the area where the initial segment connects to the cell body. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What are Nissl bodies?","choices":["The specific region joining the axon to the neuron's cell body","The cytoskeleton framework of the neuron cell","The distal ends of the axon together with its branching collaterals","Clusters of free ribosomes (and rough ER) within the neuron"],"answer":3,"exp":"Nissl bodies are clusters of free ribosomes (clusters of rough endoplasmic reticulum and free ribosomes). — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What are telodendria and axon terminals?","choices":["Telodendria are described as clusters of ribosomes; axon terminals are described as bundles of neurofilaments","Telodendria are the branches that arise off the axon; axon terminals are described as the cytoplasm within the axon","Telodendria are the ends of the axon and its collaterals; axon terminals are the ends of the telodendria","Telodendria and axon terminals are alternate names for the cell body itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"Telodendria are the ends of the axon and its collaterals, and axon terminals are the ends of the telodendria. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What are astrocytes, and what do they form?","choices":["The largest and most numerous CNS glial cells; make up the blood-brain barrier","Cells that line the fluid-filled passageways of the brain and play a role in CSF production","Cells responsible for forming the myelin sheath around axons of the central nervous system","Phagocytic cells that migrate through tissue and remove cellular waste products"],"answer":0,"exp":"Astrocytes are the largest and most numerous CNS neuroglia, and they make up the blood-brain barrier (BBB). — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What do oligodendrocytes do, and what is the difference between white and gray matter?","choices":["Oligodendrocytes produce CSF within the ventricles; white and gray matter are functionally the same tissue","Oligodendrocytes form the myelin sheath and internodes; areas with mostly myelinated axons are white matter, areas devoid of myelinated axons are gray matter","Oligodendrocytes are classified as phagocytic cells; white matter is said to contain cell bodies while gray matter contains myelinated axons","Oligodendrocytes form the blood-brain barrier at capillaries; white matter is entirely devoid of axons"],"answer":1,"exp":"Oligodendrocytes form the myelin sheath and myelin sheath gaps (internodes); areas with mostly myelinated axons are called white matter, while areas devoid of myelinated axons (composed of cell bodies and dendrites) are called gray matter. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What are microglia, and what is their function?","choices":["Cells that produce myelin sheaths wrapped tightly around CNS axons","Star-shaped glial cells responsible for forming the blood-brain barrier","Phagocytic cells that remove unwanted substances as they move through nervous tissue","Cuboidal epithelial-type cells that line the fluid-filled passageways of the CNS"],"answer":2,"exp":"Microglia are phagocytic cells that move through nervous tissue, removing unwanted substances. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What are ependymal cells, and what do they form?","choices":["Phagocytic cells that remove waste from nervous tissue while migrating throughout the entire CNS","Simple cuboidal epithelial cells lining fluid-filled passageways, involved in CSF production; they make up a cellular lining called the ependyma","Cells that form the myelin sheath around CNS axons, wrapping their membrane repeatedly around each segment","Star-shaped cells anchoring themselves to capillaries, together forming the blood-brain barrier structure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Ependymal cells are simple cuboidal epithelial cells that line fluid-filled passageways within the brain and spinal cord, are involved in CSF production, and make up a cellular lining called the ependyma. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What are the two types of neuroglia in the PNS, and what does each surround?","choices":["Satellite cells (surround cell bodies) and Schwann cells (surround axons)","The PNS contains a single type of neuroglia: the Schwann cell","Astrocytes, which surround cell bodies, and oligodendrocytes, which surround axons","Microglia, which surround cell bodies, and ependymal cells, which surround axons"],"answer":0,"exp":"There are two types of neuroglia in the PNS: satellite cells, which surround cell bodies, and Schwann cells, which surround axons. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is another name for Schwann cells, and what do they consist of?","choices":["Also called ependymal cells; consist of cilia lining passageways","Also called satellite cells; consist of the perikaryon region","Also referred to as astrocytes; composed solely of the myelin sheath itself","Also called neurolemmocytes; consist of the axolemma/neurolemma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Schwann cells are also called neurolemmocytes, and they consist of the axolemma/neurolemma. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is an internode, and what are nodes (of Ranvier)?","choices":["Internodes and nodes only exist within the CNS structures, never appearing in the PNS at all","Both terms are said to describe the exact same portion of the myelinated peripheral axon segment","An internode is the portion of a myelinated axon between two successive Schwann cells; nodes are the small gaps separating internodes","An internode refers to the small gap located between adjacent Schwann cells; nodes refer to the myelinated segments themselves"],"answer":2,"exp":"An internode is the portion of a myelinated nerve axon between two successive Schwann cells, and nodes (or nodes of Ranvier) are the small gaps that separate internodes. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why does it take many Schwann cells to myelinate an entire axon?","choices":["Each Schwann cell myelinates only about 1 mm of an axon","Each Schwann cell myelinates 10 cm of axon","Schwann cells never myelinate axons, only oligodendrocytes do in the PNS","Schwann cells myelinate the entire axon length individually, requiring only one"],"answer":0,"exp":"Because each Schwann cell myelinates only about 1 mm of an axon, it takes many Schwann cells to myelinate an entire axon. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Can a single Schwann cell myelinate multiple unmyelinated axons?","choices":["No — unmyelinated axons have no associated Schwann cells shielding them at any point along their length","Yes — a single Schwann cell shields multiple unmyelinated axons within superficial grooves, though a chain of Schwann cells is still needed to enclose an entire axon","No — a single Schwann cell can only ever be associated with one axon at a time, myelinated or not","Yes — a single Schwann cell is said to myelinate the entire length of several separate axons entirely alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"A single Schwann cell can shield multiple unmyelinated axons within superficial grooves, though a chain of Schwann cells is still needed to enclose an entire axon. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"During myelination of a peripheral axon, what happens as the Schwann cell rotates around the axon?","choices":["The inner membranous layers are compressed and the cytoplasm is forced into more superficial layers, leaving the myelin sheath as phospholipid bilayers with the Schwann cell nucleus/cytoplasm at the surface","The Schwann cell instead divides into two entirely separate daughter cells that wrap the axon, each forming its own distinct segment","The axon itself is said to rotate around a stationary Schwann cell, wrapping its own membrane around the cell repeatedly as it turns","The myelin sheath is said to form entirely from the axon's own plasma membrane, without any Schwann cell involvement whatsoever occurring"],"answer":0,"exp":"As the Schwann cell rotates around the axon, the inner membranous layers are compressed and the cytoplasm is forced into more superficial layers; when completed, the myelin sheath consists only of the phospholipid bilayers of the plasma membrane, with the Schwann cell nucleus and cytoplasm at the surface. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is a synapse, and what are the two major types?","choices":["The junction between a neuron and another cell; chemical (vesicular) and electrical (nonvesicular) synapses","A structure found only within the cell body, not at any junction between cells","Specifically the junction formed between two axons; classified as excitatory or inhibitory synapses","Specifically the junction formed between a neuron and bone tissue; classified as myelinated or unmyelinated synapses"],"answer":0,"exp":"A synapse is the junction between a neuron and another cell, and the two major types are the chemical synapse (vesicular) and the electrical synapse (nonvesicular). — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"In the CNS, what is a \"center,\" and what is it called if it has a distinct boundary?","choices":["A synapse; when it has a distinct boundary, it is instead referred to as a node","A bundle of axons; when it has a distinct boundary, it is instead referred to as a tract","A ganglion; when it has a distinct boundary, it is instead referred to as a column","A collection of cell bodies; if it has a distinct boundary, it is called a nucleus"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the CNS, a collection of cell bodies is called a center; if this center has a distinct boundary, it is called a nucleus. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"In the CNS, what do tracts and columns refer to?","choices":["Ganglia found in the PNS","Collections of cell bodies with a distinct boundary","Collections of cell bodies without a distinct boundary","Bundles of axons that make up white matter"],"answer":3,"exp":"White matter in the CNS consists of axons bundled together in tracts and columns. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"In the PNS, what are axons bundled together to form, and what are collections of cell bodies called?","choices":["Axons are bundled together to form columns; cell bodies cluster together to form centers","There is no organizational bundling structure in the PNS","Axons are bundled together to form tracts; cell bodies cluster together to form nuclei","Axons bundle into nerves; cell bodies are clustered in ganglia"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the PNS, axons are bundled together to form nerves, while collections of cell bodies are clustered in ganglia. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What are interneurons, and where are they located?","choices":["Located entirely in the PNS, connecting sensory receptors to effectors directly without CNS involvement","Located only within the ganglia, never appearing within either the CNS or PNS proper structures","Located entirely in the CNS, situated between motor and sensory neurons, analyzing sensory input and coordinating motor outputs","Functionally the same as afferent neurons, simply carrying a different descriptive name"],"answer":2,"exp":"Interneurons are located entirely in the CNS, situated between the motor and sensory neurons, analyzing sensory input and coordinating motor outputs; they can be excitatory or inhibitory. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the perikaryon, and what are axoplasm and collaterals?","choices":["Perikaryon, axoplasm, and collaterals all refer to the identical single structure","Perikaryon is the neuron cytoplasm (of the cell body); axoplasm is the cytoplasm of the axon; collaterals are branches off the axon","Perikaryon is instead classified as a type of neuroglia, not considered a true neuron structure at all","Perikaryon refers to the axon's cytoplasm; axoplasm refers to the cell body's cytoplasm; collaterals refer to the axon terminals"],"answer":1,"exp":"The perikaryon is the neuron cytoplasm, axoplasm is the cytoplasm of the axon specifically, and collaterals are branches off the axon. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What are anaxonic neurons?","choices":["Neurons with more than two processes, where axons cannot be distinguished from dendrites","Neurons possessing exactly two processes, positioned on either side of the cell body","Neurons possessing a single elongated process, with the cell body positioned off to one side","Neurons found exclusively in the PNS, never the CNS at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Anaxonic neurons have more than two processes, but axons cannot be distinguished from dendrites. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 8"}]};
